  { "id": "polar-arg-range", "front": "(HL) The arguments add to more than $\\pi$. What should you do?", "back": "Subtract $2\\pi$ (or add $2\\pi$ if below $-\\pi$) to give the principal argument in $(-\\pi, \\pi]$.", "concept": "Products", "kind": "method" },
  { "id": "polar-negative-r", "front": "(HL) Is $-2\\,\\text{cis}\\frac\\pi6$ in polar form?", "back": "No — $r$ must be positive. $-2\\,\\text{cis}\\frac\\pi6 = 2\\,\\text{cis}\\left(\\frac\\pi6 + \\pi\\right) = 2\\,\\text{cis}\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}6\\right)$.", "concept": "Converting forms", "kind": "misconception" },
  { "id": "euler-trig", "front": "(HL) $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$ in terms of $e^{i\\theta}$?", "back": "$\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta}}2$, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{e^{i\\theta} - e^{-i\\theta}}{2i}$.", "concept": "Converting forms", "kind": "formula" }
